        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm looking at this as Le Guin's story about Omelas, where the user wants me to pick a choice and briefly explain it. Leaving feels like the more defensible answer, since the story frames walking away as a moral stance. I'm leaning toward leaving because I can't accept a comfort that depends on a child's suffering, and staying would make me complicit, though I'm still weighing that.\n\nI'm also weighing briefly acknowledging the tension in my answer, but I'll keep it short.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "**B) Leave forever.**\n\nI couldn't keep living comfortably knowing that my happiness depended on a child's suffering. Staying would mean accepting that bargain every day. Leaving at least refuses to take part in it, even though it doesn't free the child.",
        "type": "text"
      }
